it/accepted-branch encryption/decryption path in both actual Node and actual Chromium. If cross-runtime or serialization support fails, retain the result and make the explicit interim decision; do not substitute a mock or claim browser support from P0 smoke tests.\n\nUse a separately named probe:ordered-mls (or comparably clear development command) whose success means these bounded observations reproduced, with a public evidence verifier and exact required-case profile. Do not enable full test:crypto-feasibility or gate:protocol; their G1–G5 status remains unpassed. New skipped/absent required evidence must not become success. Retain prior successful/failed attempts instead of overwriting them. An honest blocked/change/stop artifact with runnable failure evidence is acceptable if this candidate fails, and must not be made green by weakening requirements.\n\nUpdate docs/crypto-feasibility.md (and docs/toolchain.md / NOTICE as necessary) with source/API choice, provisional-state ownership and lifecycle, actual authenticated proof/order fixture definitions, trust and metadata limits, reproduced outcomes and a bounded continue/change/stop recommendation. Mark unsolved full G1–G5 and production decisions plainly. No unrotated shared-key fallback, claim of Marmot interoperability, owner-admission bypass, claimed archive recovery, or sequencer confidentiality validation. Keep runtime host and Worker production paths unchanged. Do not edit notes/**, plans/** or README.md; these remain owned by the Atseq documentation task/coordinator. Allowed scope is development probes/configurations/tests/fixtures, small supporting scripts/package pins, the named crypto/toolchain docs and NOTICE. CI changes may only add bounded probe verification without weakening existing P0 jobs.\n\nClaim with your own builder promise, publish every changed path as an exact-head artifact, request noseq-reviewer's independent review, address findings without lowering accepted criteria, requester-ratify t